WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.140
Why does Cook Levin encode

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.180
every NP computation as SAT?

3
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.120
The theorem says SAT is

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.600
NP-complete, so we need membership

5
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.000
in NP and hardness for

6
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.980
every NP language. Its key

7
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.400
idea is to describe an

8
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.960
entire accepting computation with Boolean

9
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.280
variables and constraints, rather than

10
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.860
search all nondeterministic branches explicitly.

11
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.200
SAT membership is straightforward: a

12
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.260
certificate assigns truth values to

13
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.060
all variables. A deterministic verifier

14
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.900
evaluates the clauses in polynomial

15
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.300
time and accepts exactly a

16
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.800
satisfying assignment. Hardness is the

17
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.380
substantial part, because infinitely many

18
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.540
different NP languages must be

19
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.340
covered by one general construction

20
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:43.740
method.

21
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.360
Fix an arbitrary NP language

22
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.800
and a nondeterministic machine M

23
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.680
deciding it within polynomial time

24
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.380
T of the input length.

25
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.640
Use a suitable standard one-tape

26
00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.340
form with a polynomial bound

27
00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.600
at least as large as

28
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.360
the input. The reduction may

29
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.600
depend on this fixed machine,

30
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.540
but its variable input is

31
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.920
the particular string x.

32
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.840
A tableau is a grid

33
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.340
of configurations. Each row records

34
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.900
one time and each column

35
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.720
one tape location, with enough

36
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.260
cells for all positions the

37
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.720
bounded computation can visit. We

38
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.040
use configuration times zero through

39
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.200
T and transition times zero

40
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.420
through T minus one, keeping

41
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.940
those endpoint roles distinct.

42
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.740
The course uses four indicator

43
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.560
families. T records the symbol

44
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.600
in a given cell at

45
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.340
a time, H records the

46
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.320
head position, S records the

47
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.160
current state, and I records

48
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.600
the selected instruction. A satisfying

49
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.900
assignment chooses all these facts,

50
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.160
including one possible nondeterministic transition

51
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.040
at each step.

52
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.700
Constraints first require a correctly

53
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.800
initialized row and a unique

54
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.440
state, head and symbol in

55
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.940
each appropriate place. Other groups

56
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.960
require an accepting time and

57
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.380
one instruction per simulated transition.

58
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.360
Finally constrain unchanged non-head cells

59
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.920
and the valid read, write,

60
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.860
state and movement effects of

61
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.100
each selected instruction.

62
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.840
An accepting machine branch supplies

63
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.940
a satisfying assignment by recording

64
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.400
what actually happens in every

65
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.280
row. The initial row is

66
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.400
correct, each move obeys the

67
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.720
machine and some row accepts.

68
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:19.680
If the branch ends early,

69
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.720
pad its accepting configuration by

70
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.360
an explicitly defined absorbing convention

71
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.140
in the encoding.

72
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.760
Conversely, a satisfying assignment determines

73
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.560
one well-defined configuration per row.

74
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.920
The initial and local-transition constraints

75
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.140
force those rows to be

76
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.420
a genuine computation, and the

77
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.480
accepting condition supplies an accepting

78
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.500
branch. Leaving out unchanged-cell constraints

79
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.260
would allow tape contents to

80
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.960
change magically far from the

81
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.880
head, breaking that reconstruction.

82
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.820
The formula has polynomially many

83
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.460
variables and a polynomial-size explicit

84
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.640
encoding for fixed M. The

85
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.740
reduction writes constraints without first

86
00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.820
solving whether M accepts x,

87
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.920
because satisfiability itself chooses the

88
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.520
branch. Nondeterminism is represented existentially

89
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.580
by the assignment, not eliminated

90
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.900
by an exponential deterministic branch

91
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.140
search inside the mapping.

92
00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.500
The course’s detailed pairwise head-uniqueness

93
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.060
encoding needs a careful polynomial-size

94
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.860
count, which the next lesson

95
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.660
develops. The theorem requires polynomial

96
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.300
size, not an unjustified tight

97
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.160
quadratic claim. A different local-window

98
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.440
encoding can have a different

99
00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.220
count, but its rules must

100
00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.600
be analyzed on their own

101
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:32.940
terms.

102
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.920
Things to take note of.

103
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.980
Fix an arbitrary polynomial-time nondeterministic

104
00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.440
machine, encode its bounded computation

105
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.160
and prove both directions of

106
00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.880
the satisfiability equivalence. State time

107
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.440
and tape boundaries, handle early

108
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.580
acceptance and justify a polynomial

109
00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.020
construction; that general method is

110
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.780
what covers every language in

111
00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:55.280
NP.
